# Rocketship stories

Copy and voice: `rocketship-story-copy.mdc`. Reasons: `_planning/component-review-decisions.md`.

## Files

- Astro: `apps/astro/src/stories/<Name>.stories.tsx`, the docs page `apps/astro/src/stories/components/<Name>Default.astro` and its example `apps/astro/src/stories/components/<Name>DefaultExample.astro`.
- Svelte: `apps/svelte/src/stories/<Name>.stories.ts` and `apps/svelte/src/stories/components/<Name>Default.svelte`.
- Every story file sets `parameters: { a11y: { disable: false } }`.
- Astro story files import the wrapper plus each library component it uses: `import '@labcat2020/rocketship/components/Stack'`.
- Wrapper components live only in `apps/*/src/stories/components/`, never in a package.

## Titles

- `Base/`: Container, Grid, Stack, Scroll, Button, Typography. Add new `Base/` stories to the sort order in `.storybook/preview.ts`.
- `Components/`: every other component.
- `Foundations/`: tokens.
- Titles are spaced words: `Components/File Input`, not `Components/FileInput`.
- A Svelte story uses the same title as its Astro story.
- A component with a second story is listed in the sort order with `Default` first: `'Disclosure', ['Default', 'Accordion']`.

## Wrappers

- Import only library components: `@labcat2020/rocketship` (Astro) or `@labcat2020/rocketship-svelte` (Svelte).
- Put examples in a `<Stack>` inside one `<Container size="content" padded>`. Stacks may nest; Containers never act as spacers. No `as="main"`.
- No `<style>` blocks, no story-only CSS, no story-only classes. If content needs a look, add it to the library.

## Docs pages (Astro)

Storybook is the docs site, so a component story is a docs page, like the Foundations stories. Reference: `DisclosureDefault.astro`.

- One `<Container size="content" padded>` holding a `<Stack gap="large">`, in this order:
  1. `<Typography as="article">` with an `<h1>` (the component or aspect) and a short usage description.
  2. The live example: `<<Name><Aspect>Example />`.
  3. `<Typography as="article">` with `<h2 id="usage-heading">Usage</h2>` and the example's source, imported with `?raw`: `<pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="usage-heading"><code>{exampleSource}</code></pre>`. The code block scrolls on narrow screens, so it is a named focusable region.
  4. On `Default` only: the API as library `<Table>`s, each named by its caption: props and attributes (name, type, default, description), slots, CSS hooks (property, token default).
- The example file holds only the component markup and its imports, so the usage code is exactly what renders.
- Keep the tables accurate: update them with every prop, slot or hook change.

## Scope and names

- Each story file exports one story and shows one aspect.
- A second aspect (sizes, gaps) is a second file: `<Name>.<aspect>.stories.tsx` exporting `<Aspect>`, wrapper `<Name><Aspect>`.
- States are not aspects. Disabled, required and error examples sit beside the default in the same story.
- Name the export for what it shows: one example `Default`; a comparison `Variants`, `States` or `<Aspect>`.
- A control with several states exports `States` (Select). A component whose only extra state is native behaviour exports `Default` (Fieldset).

## Content

- Titles are real `<h2>` elements with the `__title` class.
- Body text is in block elements (`<p>`), never bare text.
- Use realistic data in the library's voice (builds, deployments, releases) that uses every class: Table's story has numbers for `--numeric` and a totals row.
- Show a live-region `role` only where the example is genuinely dynamic.
- Forms: every control has a `<Label for>`. Error examples set `aria-invalid="true"` and `aria-describedby` on the control. A story with a required asterisk also shows "Fields marked * are required."
- Compose forms as in `.agents/skills/rocketship-component/references/patterns.md` → Forms.
- Every attribute does what the copy says. No markup with no effect (a `<datalist>` on a styled range).
- An error example shows a rule the control cannot express. Rule out impossible values with `min`, `max` or `accept` instead of reporting them.
- Use library controls only. If a control is still under review, the example waits; no native stand-ins.
- A state only script can set (`indeterminate`) gets a one-line `<script is:inline>` in the wrapper. Never fake it with a class. If the story host does not run the script, leave the example out.
- No `autofocus`.
